<$fill $name="body">

!! Pieces

|!Tiddler |!Role |
|`layer.js` |The `<$drop-attach-layer>` widget, rendered once from `layer` (PageTemplate). Page-wide listeners in the capture phase: file drags over targets, drops, clicks (select), paste |
|`attach.js` |Library: which files are attached (`typeOf`), replace targets and replaced fields (`isReplaceTarget`, `replaceFields`), the size limit, the new title (`titleFor`, `uniqueTitle`), the fields, the action tiddlers, the target specs. No DOM, covered by the specs |
|`startup.js` |Registers the extra file types from the config as base64 types, in the browser and on the server |
|`styles-target` |Stylesheet that marks the selected paste target, generated from the target specs |
|`targets/*` |Drop target specs (story tiddler, stream row) |
|`actions/append-view` |The shipped after-upload action: appends `<<attachment>>` for the new tiddler to the target's text; config `append` |
|`card` |Global procedure `<<attachment>>`: the attachment card (wikitext only) |
|`templates/modal`, `templates/download` |The card's "view large" modal and download content |
|`templates/canonical-uri` |`_canonical_uri` for extracted files |

!! Flow of a drop

```
file dragged over a target ──► target gets da-dragover; event stopped (no core overlay)
drop                       ──► one binary file onto a binary tiddler → confirm → replace its content (see below)
                               else files split: binary → attach, text → core import (tm-import-tiddlers)
                               too big → notice
each binary file           ──► FileReader (base64) → tiddler {title, type, text, drop-attach.file}
                           ──► every action tiddler, with <target> <new> <filename> <type> <size> <source>
                           ──► one notice
```

!! Replacing the content

`isReplaceTarget`: an existing non-system tiddler with `_canonical_uri` or a base64 type. `replaceFields` keeps every field and sets `text` (base64), `type` (the file's), `modified`; it removes `_canonical_uri`. Only when that value started with `files/` and there is no `tw-desk-update-of` yet, it is copied into `tw-desk-update-of`, so a second replace before TW Desk ran keeps the original path. External URIs get no marker. `drop-attach.file` is updated where it exists. Paste never replaces.

!! Why the capture phase

TiddlyWiki's page template is one big `$dropzone`: it highlights on `dragenter` and opens `$:/Import` on `drop`. The layer listens on the document in the capture phase and stops the drag events (`dragenter`, `dragover`, `dragleave`, `drop`) when they happen inside a target, so they never reach the page dropzone. Outside targets nothing is stopped and the core behaves as usual. Only drags that carry files are looked at: tiddler drags (links, ports, the story) are untouched.

!! Paste and the selected target

A click inside a target writes its title to `$:/temp/rimir/drop-attach/target` (a temp tiddler: not saved, gone after a reload). `styles-target` renders one CSS rule from it, `<selector>[<title-attribute>="<title>"]` per target spec, drawing a 3px bar as a background image (no pseudo-element, no positioning, so it works on any target element). A paste is handled when the clipboard holds files and the paste does not happen in a text editor (textarea or contenteditable; TiddlyWiki's editor imports pasted files itself). A one-line input such as the sidebar search, which has focus after loading, does not count as an editor. The selected target must be visible on the page; otherwise the notice asks to select one.

!! Types

`typeOf` takes the file's MIME type, else the one for its extension. A type TiddlyWiki knows is attached if TiddlyWiki stores it as base64 (`$tw.config.contentTypeInfo[type].encoding`), else it goes to the import. A type TiddlyWiki does not know is attached as `application/octet-stream`, unless it is a `text/…` type. The extra types are registered at startup with `$tw.utils.registerFileType(type, "base64", extension)`, so they are attached under their own type and a Node server saves them with their own extension.

